        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          You can caulk over old caulk only in a narrow set of cases, and most of the time you should not. New silicone bonds poorly to cured silicone and almost nothing else will stick to silicone at all, while latex or acrylic caulk over old latex caulk can work if the old bead is sound, clean, dry and the new bead overlaps onto bare surface on both sides. In bathrooms and kitchens the bigger problem is what is under the old bead: mold, soap scum and trapped moisture that a fresh layer simply seals in. For any wet joint, removing the old caulk and starting clean is the job that actually lasts, and it takes less time than people expect.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Doesn't New Caulk Stick Well to Old Caulk?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Caulk is designed to bond to the materials on either side of a joint, such as tile, acrylic, porcelain, wood and painted trim. It is not designed to bond to itself once cured. Old caulk has a smooth, often slightly contaminated skin of soap, body oils, cleaning products and dust, and the new bead is being asked to grip that skin rather than a clean, porous surface. Even when the two look fused, the bond line between them is the weak point.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a geometry problem. A good caulk joint is a bead that bridges a gap and holds onto both sides, flexing as the materials move. When you run a new bead over an old one, the new caulk sits on a rounded hump. It ends up thin at the edges, where it meets the tile or tub, and thick over the old bead in the middle. The thin edges are exactly where it needs to be strongest, and they are the first place it peels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture makes everything worse. Old caulk in a shower has usually already let some water past it, either through hairline cracks or where its edges lifted. A new layer on top does not dry that water out. It traps it against the joint, where it feeds mold and keeps the new caulk's edges from bonding as well as they would to dry surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the usual result of caulking over caulk is a joint that looks great for a few weeks, then starts to lift at the edges, shows dark spots through the new bead, and needs redoing within months. That is why the honest short answer is: you can, in limited cases, but it is rarely worth the saving. Pulling out the old bead is the job that actually fixes the joint.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
