        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Caulking over caulk works in one realistic situation: sound, clean, dry latex caulk on painted trim, topped with a fresh bead that overlaps bare surface on both sides. New silicone bonds poorly to old silicone, nothing else bonds to silicone at all, and anything laid over moldy bathroom caulk simply seals the mold in.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For tubs, showers, sinks and any joint that has actually failed, the right answer is removal. Cut out the old bead, clean off the silicone film, treat and dry the joint, then lay a fresh bead of the right caulk and let it cure fully. It costs an extra hour or two and buys years more than any shortcut.
          </p>
        </div>

